pollution. Specially made bio organisms made by Mega Intelligence and such allow the solving of toxic run off from factories.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12952-accelerator","name":"Accelerator","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-12-05T17:07:46-08:00","id":1427408,"title":"","content":"We know from Aeon that robotic servants, smart vehicles, and direct neural data interface eventually proliferate in the Nova Age (hence the Possession Crisis), but I don\u2019t think those are proliferating for consumers yet in 2028.<br /> <br /> I\u2019d say immersive vr and realistic holograms set ups are still relatively expensive but spreading entertainment and communication set ups in homes (note - without neural interface, immersive vr doesn\u2019t create tactile illusions, but there\u2019s a market for... what everyone knows are sex suites but aren\u2019t openly marketed as such)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-12-13T12:11:19-08:00","id":1428290,"title":"","content":"Home assistants for the elderly and ill. Which is to say, cheap sensors that you can place though your entire house, with a learning system that doesn't just go: oh, she fell in the bathroom, but can say: Wait am minute, over the lats 30 minutes her actions have diverged from the norm--better call for a safety check. <br /> <br /> This would be in addition to sensors picking up things like heart attacks and such.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1458-newk","name":"NewK","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"360"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-12-13T17:32:32-08:00","id":1428306,"title":"","content":"In the FSA, home assistants would not look human. We have seen too many Terminator movies. Nihonjin consumers had a diet of Astro Boy and 8 Man, so they would be fine with androids.<br /> <br /> Smart phones could be replaced with smart glasses. We are developing electronics that could be put on clear glass right now that could create full color images. Imagine a device that could be used to make phone calls, function as a voice activated computer, make credit card purchases, transmit and receive social media feeds in Augmented Reality, and knows all that you have done and where you are at all times. The fascist, cyberpunk dystopian ruling elite of the FSA would love it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":8102,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8102-doctor-zero","user":"Doctor Zero","date":". 12-14-2020, 02:58 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8102-doctor-zero","name":"Doctor Zero","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"28"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-12-13T21:07:51-08:00","id":1428316,"title":"","content":"Hand-held ultrasound and MRI scanners, or the equivalents, for fast and easy testing. Also the test that works from a single drop of blood, only it works.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12952-accelerator","name":"Accelerator","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-12-14T08:32:57-08:00","id":1428348,"title":"","content":"I love this conversation. <br /> <br /> So I was thinking about the ways such tech could go. One version is everything is super visible, like the tech is all shiny and new and looks that way. Another version is that the tech is hidden behind false walls, pop up panels, and designed with a facade of more rustic or historic design. <br /> <br /> I always had the interesting image of someone going into what looks like a stone cabin with a simple wood burning fireplace and so looks like it lacks much things. Only for the owner to have hidden dishwashers and ovens and microwaves and toasters and fridges and freezers and tv units and all that - just without seeming to look like they do.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/661-lordheru","name":"LordHeru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar661_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1100"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-12-14T09:44:08-08:00","id":1428360,"title":"","content":"It might be an aesthetic thing.<br /> <br /> People once thought spaceship people would be nude. This would be comparatively normal.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12952-accelerator","name":"Accelerator","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-12-14T20:12:33-08:00","id":1428430,"title":"","content":"One example would be spray on 'invisible&quot; PV panels that can allow nearly every home to become energy independent. Which mind you, would very much fit into the trends in Aeon of people moving out to the backwoods an setting up their own communities--it was accelerated by the Aberrant war, but in truth, the trends were already moving that way before the war. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1458-newk","name":"NewK","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"360"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-12-14T23:58:05-08:00","id":1428441,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>NewK</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-aberrant/1426818-aberrant-what-sorts-of-new-consumer-technology-exists-in-2028?p=1428430#post1428430\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">One example would be spray on 'invisible&amp;quot; PV panels that can allow nearly every home to become energy independent. Which mind you, would very much fit into the trends in Aeon of people moving out to the backwoods an setting up their own communities--it was accelerated by the Aberrant war, but in truth, the trends were already moving that way before the war. </div> </div> </div> </div>Another variant of this is the usage of specialised self replicating Inks and gels to set up electrical lines","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12952-accelerator","name":"Accelerator","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-12-15T19:21:53-08:00","id":1428542,"title":"","content":"A minor toy with some interesting uses is a popular hit. It's basically glow-in-the-dark Silly Putty. It's fairly bright, no flashlight, but useful. Along the way a Nova discovered that people connected to Quantum can make Glow Putty glow far more brightly if they concentrate on it. It's still just a toy, the the sneaky can make it very useful.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-12-15T23:46:14-08:00","id":1428564,"title":"","content":"I love this conversation too. Allowing myself to feel hope for the future which was not something I allowed myself for the past several years.<br /> <br /> How about some inventions from Star Trek like a universal translation device, or a medical tricorder? Also, upper class people could probably travel by space ship and spend time in an orbital space hotels.<br /> <br /> Also, self driving cars could be available. Musk talked about creating an automatic taxi service to compete with Uber and Lyft. People would lease a taxi service, for certain amount of miles per month instead of owning a car.<br /> <br /> A Nova could invent true molecular nanotechnology like in Engines of Creation that could change the world.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8102-doctor-zero","name":"Doctor Zero","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"28"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-30T10:23:47-07:00","id":1450327,"title":"","content":"You know there's something I myself have just realised in a convo about mechanising assembly lines.<br /> <br /> Much better sensors and linked wireless. Not just in terms of battery power but in terms of multi tasking and sheer accuracy. Stuff that would normally cost thousands to make and calibrate now has cheaper substitutes for rare earth metals and helpful AI to make costs drop by like a tenth.<br /> <br /> I'll also like to see more nanoscale tech. Not just the weird shit like clouds of utility fog but more like instead of depending on exotic metals and high pressures and temperatures there's a focus on using highly sophisticated biotech and nanoscale structures to pull off efficiencies and flexibilities that would be bullshit before.<br /> <br /> I should probably expand on this later. Someone put a like or a quote if you're interested.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12952-accelerator","name":"Accelerator","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-30T14:55:18-07:00","id":1450360,"title":"","content":"Alright fine. Technology: <br /> <br /> Automating processes has always been a mainstay of the industrial revolution, but what most people don't quite get is how expensive it can get. Most Capitalists required a pre-existing fortune to invest in, or a bank loan, or... you know, the various problems in a capitalist society which means that having wealth means you can more easily accumulate more wealth, and so on and so forth. This is because automation is frickin' hard. Most people don't quite get how complex the human brain, mind, hand to eye coordination, and flexible neurological program is. You get ordered to put in a screw in a particular component. You do that, pick it up, and then put it down. An automated system needs something that would automatically replace each screw when placed into the component, sensors and range finders to ensure that you actually move the screw to the right area, and of course an error checking mechanism that ensure that this won't gum up in like, after the first 600 screws. Maybe a modular set of machine tools, manipulators, sensors, and computers, not too dissimilar to lego or kinex, would let anyone, and I mean anyone, create any machine or assembly line they desire, powered by broadcasted power from super-efficient solar panels or other rot. And they will hold plans to produce anything they want. <br /> <br /> At long last, the means of production will be given to *everyone*. It's not those nanoforges you see in things like Eclipse Phase, but those are things more suited to the year 3000. <br /> <br /> Biotech has always been an interest to me. Biological cells and enzymes, capable of using massive scale step by step pathways that lets them produce and manipulate substances of mind-boggling complexity. All of them without the need for high temperatures, pressures, deadly acids, or precious metal catalysts. There are near to zero pathways involving the heavy metals, but that is as much lack of evolution as limits to biology. Synthetic microbes with a few added bonuses to extend their range of metabolism, along with either automated gene editing system or a machine-microbe interface that allows the onboard computer control the damned synthetic microbes in the nutrient bath, along with several taps that allow the pumping and control of any substance. Ranging from complex proteins, to antibiotics, paints, to nitroglycerin, to cleaning fluid, to gasoline, to coffee. As long as the bare atoms are there, it's all good. Chemistry first started from singular alchemists experimenting in lonely labs, to massive factories with huge reactors and humming of giant machines, to once again the lone singular chemist with a do-it-all chemistry set.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12952-accelerator","name":"Accelerator","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-30T15:17:37-07:00","id":1450363,"title":"","content":"That's all quite nifty and interesting and showcases all the ways things can be effected by the growing technologies. I mean even something like more advanced 3D printing would be cool. <br /> <br /> One option is to have it so the current mundane stuff is super-automated while the various nova designed stuff is a miss-match of semi-labor intensive and fully labor intensive operations. Thus why some basic things are now built entirely by machines other things still need human labor. <br /> <br /> Also finding various new and improved ways to gather resources in ways that are more environmentally friendly are also very cool. <br /> <br /> All the stuff there showcases how many changes can occur and the nature of the changing world.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/661-lordheru","name":"LordHeru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar661_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1100"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-30T15:39:09-07:00","id":1450368,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LordHeru</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-aberrant/1426818-aberrant-what-sorts-of-new-consumer-technology-exists-in-2028?p=1450363#post1450363\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's all quite nifty and interesting and showcases all the ways things can be effected by the growing technologies. I mean even something like more advanced 3D printing would be cool.<br /> <br /> One option is to have it so the current mundane stuff is super-automated while the various nova designed stuff is a miss-match of semi-labor intensive and fully labor intensive operations. Thus why some basic things are now built entirely by machines other things still need human labor.<br /> <br /> Also finding various new and improved ways to gather resources in ways that are more environmentally friendly are also very cool.<br /> <br /> All the stuff there showcases how many changes can occur and the nature of the changing world.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ah, I forgot another thing.<br /> <br /> Computer chips, mass grown using biotech that breaks down electronic waste and rebuilds it onto a reconfigurable pattern within the 3D printer in an aqueous solution at room temperature. It will never be as mighty as nova-made quantum chips, neither will it be able to match the sheer technological growth or volume of a dedicated factory. <br /> <br /> What this does is that it enables anyone, and I mean anyone to create their own circuits, with an OS to match inside of it that's meant to be modular and reconfigurable, chips being able to be put in parallel or series to build up to even greater powers. Inefficient, inelegant, and capable of supplying a nation with its computer chip needs without a dedicated factory or having to mine rare earth metals. They don't need superpowerful chips. They just need chips that can work the pumps and compute the algorithms which all society needs.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12952-accelerator","name":"Accelerator","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-30T16:25:00-07:00","id":1450372,"title":"","content":"You shouldn't forget to factor something reeeeeally important: <b>culture &amp; acceptance</b>.<br /> <br /> You se, we could have &quot;videophones&quot; since the 80s (or 90s) in our world, but when they got made, it was a flop because no one wanted to be seen whilst calling/being called. Only now, in a Pandemic world, people finally are accepting video calls.<br /> <br /> So, take these in consideration. <i><b>Humanity sometimes is VERY conservative, even if a new technology is better or more versatile than what's in vogue</b></i>. :-]","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1001-timecrafter","name":"Timecrafter","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1001_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"134"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-30T16:47:34-07:00","id":1450374,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Timecrafter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-aberrant/1426818-aberrant-what-sorts-of-new-consumer-technology-exists-in-2028?p=1450372#post1450372\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You shouldn't forget to factor something reeeeeally important: <b>culture &amp; acceptance</b>.<br /> <br /> You se, we could have &quot;videophones&quot; since the 80s (or 90s) in our world, but when they got made, it was a flop because no one wanted to be seen whilst calling/being called. Only now, in a Pandemic world, people finally are accepting video calls.<br /> <br /> So, take these in consideration. <i><b>Humanity sometimes is VERY conservative, even if a new technology is better or more versatile than what's in vogue</b></i>. :-]</div> </div> </div> </div>Here is the problem with that: Humans make do, often adapting to any thing they go through, attempting to wring out every single last drop of power from their environment. This is especially true in the less prosperous parts of the world.<br /> <br /> You know what happens to most E-waste? It doesn't get recycled. It can't be. Too much mercury for incineration. Too heterogenous for any kind of sorting. It just gets sent into landfills. And by that I mean landfills in 3rd world countries. There is roughly a billion dollar's worth in those electronics, waiting to be recycled and rebuilt into new ones. Just that no one can, because they're locked in really complex alloys and bits. <br /> <br /> Most forms of mining is destructive to the environment, firstly because heavy metals contaminate the water, and secondly because of the introduction of acid into the water, creating what can be called an acid lake with heavy metal ions. Cleanup is hard, dangerous, and requires expensive equipment or reagants.... or it can be done a dozen times cheaper with phyto and bacterial remediation, using plants and microbes to absorb and render harmless the deadly products of mining. Perfect for places with weak governance and lack of infrastructure and tech. <br /> <br /> The thing here is that we already see people doing shit with tech that wouldn't normally be seen. In Africa people are using cell phone minutes as an odd form of credit, because landlines require infrastructure to build and their own currency is unstable, so they use mobile minutes to buy food, pay rent, and all else. And they'll do the same with this tech. Maybe some people would be conservative with this technology... but other people wouldn't, because those people won't have the luxury of having dedicated chemical plants for pumping out the chemicals they need to survive, like herbicides or fertilizer or those antibiotics you really need but can't produce. Cheaper microchips means that they can have more computing, automation, and all those little things like computer servers. <br /> <br /> You don't need a videophone call to survive. But for a state that's low on funds and capital, and sees a chance to create fertilizer and environmental cleanup on the cheap? To be able to attempt to raise his people's standards of living without having to spend precious capital and funds, already so difficult to obtain and needed in so many other places? You bet he'll use it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12952-accelerator","name":"Accelerator","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-30T18:58:39-07:00","id":1450382,"title":"","content":"One thing that might fit the setting is improved batteries and photovoltaic cells. Cell phones that stay charged for a week. Electric cars that can travel a thousand miles on a charge and, while sitting in a parking lot, both cool and recharge themselves. Houses that sell more electricity to the grid than they use. All would be normal with the kind of tech the setting uses.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-03T09:32:45-07:00","id":1450833,"title":"","content":"Three entries of this thread in that involved elderly care and I realized that nova-advanced telomerase research would render such care irrelevant by ensuring that most of the geriatric problems do not happen. Even if human lifespan itself were not extended, the functionality of the human body would be and thus there would be little to no need for retirement homes, geriatric wards, or the like.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/765-darksol-aeternium","name":"Darksol-aeternium","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar765_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"392"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-03T10:37:21-07:00","id":1450850,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Darksol-aeternium</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-aberrant/1426818-aberrant-what-sorts-of-new-consumer-technology-exists-in-2028?p=1450833#post1450833\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Three entries of this thread in that involved elderly care and I realized that nova-advanced telomerase research would render such care irrelevant by ensuring that most of the geriatric problems do not happen. Even if human lifespan itself were not extended, the functionality of the human body would be and thus there would be little to no need for retirement homes, geriatric wards, or the like.</div> </div> </div> </div>Cool. I predict social programs for the shift in suddenly young again 80 year olds. Also, this is like, 2028. Is it really that fast?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12952-accelerator","name":"Accelerator","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-03T11:42:01-07:00","id":1450860,"title":"","content":"Not really technology, but a team of novas with Sherlock levels of scrutinizing detail/quasi-psychic edges and/or powers forming a legal team. They don't completely stop innocent people getting sent to prison or the death chamber, but they lower the odds of that happening to the lowest levels it's ever been. Some progressive county/state/region/whathaveyou even tries to fast track allowing these types of novas onto the police force to disseminate evidence and testimonies so that less people are incorrectly convicted of a crime.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26860-saveversus","name":"SaveVersus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26860_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-03T12:17:08-07:00","id":1450863,"title":"","content":"Or, if not assembling such a legal team, then creating a criminal activity monitor system on the level of what is used by Batman to root out criminal contacts in his targets. Law enforcement could track down criminal empires as much as the IRS could find federal tax evaders.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/765-darksol-aeternium","name":"Darksol-aeternium","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar765_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"392"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-03T13:46:59-07:00","id":1450879,"title":"","content":"Given the progress in drones and the battery tech in the setting aren't self-pilotting passenger drones obvious? I could really see these in New York and LA. The rich and the Upper Middle Class would fly over the traffic. The Hoi Polloi would fight the eternal gridlock.<br /> <br /> Passenger Drones would be an ultimate consumer luxury.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}